> **Rigorous Statement of the Unfreezing (Response to Reviewer 4).**
>
> *Inner product:* The physical Hilbert space is defined by the induced (Rieffel) inner product on the quotient H_phys = Ker(Ĥ_total)/Im(Ĥ_total), following the standard Dirac quantization procedure. In the Born–Oppenheimer splitting Ψ = Φ[q_ab,ψ,φ]χ(θ;q_ab), the clock factor χ is normalized in L²(S¹, dμ) with the flat measure dμ = ds/L, and the gravitational factor Φ is normalized in the induced Wheeler–DeWitt inner product (DeWitt 1967).
>
> *Domain:* The operator Ĥ_Fisher = −iE_modσ₁(∂_s − iA_Berry) is essentially self-adjoint on C^∞(S¹) with periodic boundary conditions. Its self-adjoint extension has domain H¹(S¹) (Sobolev). The total Hamiltonian Ĥ_total = Ĥ_grav + Ĥ_matter + Ĥ_Fisher is defined on the intersection of the gravitational domain (a suitable weighted Sobolev space on superspace) and H¹(S¹) ⊗ H_matter.
>
> *Normalizability:* Normalizability of χ is guaranteed by the compactness of S¹: the spectrum {E_modσ₁(2πk/L)} is discrete, and the positive-branch projection k > 0 selects a normalizable sector. Normalizability of Φ is the standard Wheeler–DeWitt normalizability condition, which we do not claim to solve in full generality (it remains open for inhomogeneous cosmologies).
>
> *Comparison with Page–Wootters (1983) and relational time:* The Born–Oppenheimer unfreezing is a known semiclassical move (Banks 1985; Brout 1987; Vilenkin 1989). What IGRT adds relative to Page–Wootters is (i) a *specific* clock variable τ_FR = α_mod s_QFI tied to the Fisher–Rao metric, (ii) a *modular* calibration E_mod = k_B T_mod, and (iii) a *covariant* coupling to the DHOST gravitational sector via Eq. (3.3). Page–Wootters leaves the clock choice arbitrary; IGRT fixes it via the synchronization postulate. What is *not* gained is a fully non-perturbative resolution of the WDW constraint — this remains open (OP-2).

